  "narrative": "No independent validation can be requested because the remaining voluntary validation budget is zero. The twelve newly delivered discovery comparisons are assessed against the 0.15 composite-dependency-unit threshold using the signed estimates and 95 percent Welch intervals.\n\nThe Ky-library conditional tests strongly support the core SMARCA4-loss, KEAP1-loss, and MSI-high associations within that library context. H147 has signed estimate +0.830 with lower bound +0.705, H148 has signed estimate +0.942 with lower bound +0.806, and H149 has signed estimate +0.981 with lower bound +0.757. All clear the scientific threshold comfortably. These results argue that the global Ky-library contrast does not remove the predictor effects and that the central associations are not merely an artifact of the dominant library context. I accept H147, H148, and H149 as supplied-dataset associations and close them.\n\nThe Cas9-activity robustness panel also supports the core effects. H153 shows a large SMARCA4-loss stronger-dependency effect in low Cas9 activity, signed estimate +0.886 with lower bound +0.780. H154 shows a large KEAP1-loss weaker-dependency effect in low Cas9 activity, signed estimate +0.880 with lower bound +0.779. H155 and H156 show that the MSI-high stronger-dependency association is substantial in both high and low Cas9 activity, signed estimates +0.696 and +0.849 with lower bounds +0.507 and +0.689. Together with the earlier accepted high-Cas9 SMARCA4 and KEAP1 results, these lower-bound clear effects support internal robustness of the core associations across the measured Cas9-activity range. I accept and close H153, H154, H155, and H156.\n\nH158 rejects the direct TP53-loss weaker-dependency hypothesis in high research_signature_b. The signed estimate is -0.224 with interval -0.495 to +0.047, so the direction is opposite the claimed direction and the interval does not support a threshold-clearing weaker-dependency effect. This is consistent with the accepted interaction evidence that the broad TP53-loss association is attenuated in high constructed-signature strata, but it should not be reinterpreted as a meaningful reverse direct effect because the interval includes zero.\n\nH159 rejects a direct PTEN-loss stronger-dependency effect in TP53-intact low research_signature_a lines. The signed estimate is +0.008 with interval -0.090 to +0.106, far below the threshold and compatible with no meaningful effect. H160 similarly rejects a direct PTEN-loss effect in TP53-intact positive research_signature_b lines, signed estimate +0.070 with interval -0.048 to +0.188. These negative boundary tests are important because they prevent extending the accepted research_signature_a-specific PTEN result to all constructed-signature contexts or to all TP53-intact backgrounds.\n\nH150 is not accepted as a threshold-clearing direct PTEN-loss effect under high Cas9 activity among TP53-intact, SMARCA4-intact lines. The signed estimate is +0.255, but the lower bound is +0.132, below the 0.15-unit threshold. It is directionally positive and close to the boundary, so the evidence supports a small positive conditional association rather than no effect, but it does not establish a scientifically meaningful PTEN-loss effect in that broad high-Cas9 context. I reject the threshold claim and close H150 as a broad direct claim. The more focused result is H152, which accepts a large interaction among TP53-intact lines: the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency comparison is substantially larger in research_signature_a at or above zero than in its complement, signed estimate +0.526 with lower bound +0.360. This is coherent with the accepted direct high-research_signature_a PTEN result H113, the independently validated core-intact high-research_signature_a result H117, and the low-signature null H159. I therefore accept H152 as a dataset-internal specificity boundary involving a constructed assay and an analysis-defined threshold, not as biological mechanism or external generalization.\n\nThe new proposals refine the PTEN audit. They test the TP53-intact high-research_signature_a direct PTEN-loss effect separately under high and low Cas9 activity, and they test the low-research_signature_a boundary under high Cas9 activity. If high-signature tests are threshold-clearing while the low-signature test is null, PTEN-loss context specificity will be further supported across editing-quality strata. If the high-signature high-Cas9 test fails despite H152, the PTEN association should be treated as narrow and possibly unstable outside the already validated core-intact or lower-Cas9 settings.\n\nAudit considerations remain important. The run includes many comparisons, so chance and selection effects are non-negligible; however, many accepted lower bounds are large enough that these specific claims are less likely to be chance-only. The research signatures and markers are constructed assays with no assigned biological role, and several PTEN conclusions depend on thresholded constructed-signature strata. The robustness findings concern measured technical strata and supplied data, not causality. I closed low-priority no-evidence probes, including tiny-cell technical or interaction probes, to keep future analysis focused on PTEN boundaries and already supported core robustness.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 appraise update. No validation budget remains. Core internal model: SMARCA4 loss strongly associated with stronger measured dependency; KEAP1 loss strongly associated with weaker measured dependency; MSI-high strongly associated with stronger measured dependency; TP53 loss associated with weaker measured dependency but attenuated in high constructed research_signature_a and research_signature_b strata. The new Ky-library tests and low/high Cas9 tests substantially increase internal robustness of SMARCA4, KEAP1, and MSI-high associations. PTEN loss remains context-specific: accepted in TP53-intact high research_signature_a settings and in selected KEAP1-loss or TP53/SMARCA4-intact contexts, but broadly subthreshold under high Cas9 without signature restriction (H150 lower bound +0.132). The accepted H152 interaction and negative H159/H160 boundary results support restriction to research_signature_a >=0 among TP53-intact lines rather than a uniform PTEN effect.\n\nNext active analyses: PTEN loss in TP53-intact high research_signature_a high Cas9, PTEN loss in TP53-intact low research_signature_a high Cas9, and PTEN loss in TP53-intact high research_signature_a low Cas9. These test whether the PTEN context specificity survives both high and low editing-quality strata and whether the low-signature complement remains null. Low-priority no-evidence claims are closed to avoid tiny-cell or redundant interaction tests. All future positive results must be interpreted as supplied-dataset associations, especially those involving constructed research_signature_a or research_signature_b thresholds; validation and causal/generalization claims remain unsupported."
