  "narrative": "No response deadlines or new validation results were due, and the independent validation budget is exhausted. Therefore no claim status changes are warranted from validation at this point. The evidence base in the supplied dataset is now sufficiently mature to state dataset-internal conclusions, while still avoiding causal or external-generalization language.\n\nBottom line: the strongest and most robust dataset-internal associations are SMARCA4 loss associated with stronger composite dependency, KEAP1 loss associated with weaker dependency, MSI-high associated with stronger dependency, and TP53 loss associated with weaker dependency. PTEN loss is not a uniform predictor; it is supported mainly in TP53-intact, research_signature_a-positive contexts, and its effect size remains context-dependent. Many other measured genomic, clinical, technical, and constructed marker variables do not show scientifically meaningful associations under the 0.15 composite dependency-score-unit threshold rule.\n\nSupported findings. Using signed 95% Welch intervals and accepting only when the signed lower bound exceeds +0.15 units in the registered direction: SMARCA4 loss is repeatedly associated with stronger dependency, H5/R5 signed 0.833 lower 0.767 upper 0.898, independently validated R13 signed 0.795 lower 0.695 upper 0.896, with accepted strata including lineage, age, screen-quality, Cas9-quality, growth-rate halves, research_signature contexts, and aneuploidy strata such as H238/R214 signed 0.851 lower 0.760 upper 0.942 and H246/R220 signed 0.814 lower 0.718 upper 0.909. KEAP1 loss is repeatedly associated with weaker dependency, H7/R7 signed 0.832 lower 0.766 upper 0.897, independently validated R26 signed 0.783 lower 0.688 upper 0.878, with analogous accepted strata including H239/R215 signed 0.809 lower 0.713 upper 0.905 and H247/R221 signed 0.854 lower 0.763 upper 0.944. MSI-high is associated with stronger dependency, H41/R75 signed 0.792 lower 0.676 upper 0.907, independently validated for a TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-intact context through H90/R87/R93 signed 0.745 lower 0.666 upper 0.825 discovery and signed 0.779 lower 0.675 upper 0.884 validation, with accepted strata across age, lineage, aneuploidy, hypoxia/EMT-related contexts, and screen-quality contexts. TP53 loss is associated overall with weaker dependency, H3/R3 signed 0.423 lower 0.327 upper 0.520, independently validated R39 signed 0.471 lower 0.334 upper 0.607, with accepted contexts including lineage main effects, low research_signature_a contexts, screen-quality strata, age-boundary strata, growth halves, and aneuploidy strata. These core claims are dataset-internal associations among measured variables or analysis-defined eligibility strata, not single-gene knockout phenotypes or causal effects.\n\nPTEN loss is context-sensitive rather than globally uniform. It is accepted in several TP53-intact and research_signature_a-related contexts, including H58/R42 validated R53, H67/R55 validated R66, H117/R121 validated R131, and growth-half contexts such as H216/R196 and H226/R197. However, global PTEN claims are not accepted as a uniform weaker- or stronger-dependency effect, and broad lineage main effects H224/R202, H225/R203, H236/R212, and H237/R213 remain borderline or unresolved. The PTEN evidence should be interpreted as dependent on background: TP53 intact status and constructed research_signature_a positivity strengthen the apparent association, while triple-intact high-aneuploidy and some high-Cas9-quality contexts remain uncertain without signature conditioning. I registered two focused PTEN probes to test whether accepted triple-intact research_signature_a-positive PTEN evidence persists when split by aneuploidy.\n\nInteraction findings. The most robust interaction pattern is attenuation of the TP53-loss weaker-dependency association in elevated constructed research_signature_a or research_signature_b contexts. Accepted examples include H104/R105 validated R120 for research_signature_a and H82/R77 validated R79 for research_signature_b. These should be described as context-dependent attenuation, not a new accepted opposite-direction main effect, because direct main effects in high-signature strata such as H76, H105, H157, and H158 do not support threshold-clearing opposite-direction associations. The research signatures are constructed assays without assigned biological pathways, so these interactions are data patterns in this dataset rather than mechanistic claims.\n\nNegative or non-supported findings. Broadly, many measured predictors do not clear the scientific threshold in their registered directions: lineage subtype H1/R1, KRAS mutation H2/R2, MTAP loss H4/R4, PIK3CA mutation H6/R6, MYC amplification H8/R8, arbitrary research markers D/E/F H9-R11/R9-R11, STK11 loss H18/R32, CDKN2A loss in global and refined probes H19/R33 and related rejected CDKN2A contexts, ARID1A loss H20/R34, RB1 loss H22/R36, BRCA2 loss H39/R38, NF1 loss H40/R74, sex H42/R76, media serum H43/R52, BRAF V600E H53/R60, EGFR amplification H54/R61, ERBB2 amplification H55/R62, SMAD4 loss H56/R63, APC mutation H96/R100 and APC-Wnt interaction H97/R101, screen batch H81/R72, library main effects H23/R37 and H33/R78, matched omics coverage H35/R92, and many lineage, library, screen-quality, ploidy, MYC-expression, growth-rate, and signature interactions that did not clear the threshold. These negative findings constrain overinterpretation but are not causal nulls and do not prove absence in other datasets.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The main unresolved boundaries are: PTEN broad TP53-intact lineage main effects H224/R202, H225/R203, H236/R212, and H237/R213; triple-intact high-aneuploidy PTEN without signature conditioning H248/R223; TP53-intact high-aneuploidy PTEN H243/R222; high-Cas9-quality triple-intact PTEN H146/R138; research_signature_c PTEN context H205/R185; and MSI-high within TP53-loss lines H93/R94/R106, where sparse cells produce wide intervals. Age-dependent claims are limited because age is missing for many lines. All stratum cutpoints are analysis-defined or sample-dependent, and unadjusted marginal contrasts cannot rule out confounding among correlated measured variables. Multiple exploratory subgroup tests were performed, so accepted stratum-specific effects should be weighted according to consistency, size, uncertainty, and validation, not as independent discoveries of equal strength.\n\nNext analysis. I registered four low-multiplicity follow-up comparisons: PTEN loss within the accepted triple-intact TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-intact, research_signature_a-positive context split by high versus low aneuploidy, to test whether PTEN evidence holds in the high-aneuploidy side; and TP53 loss within high versus low Cas9 activity, to complete a technical-quality robustness check for the TP53 core association. If further capacity remains after these, the most valuable analysis is not broad subgroup mining but a pre-specified complete-case or high-quality robustness panel and clearer separation of PTEN contexts. No causal, single-gene, mechanistic, or external-generalization claim is supported by the current evidence."
}

