  "narrative": "All nine newly delivered comparisons are valid Welch independent-cell tests with every comparison cell at least 20 observations, and all estimates/intervals are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. Applying the pre-specified dataset-internal rule that an effect is scientifically meaningful only when the signed 95 percent lower bound is clearly above +0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction: H286/R264 accepts (PTEN-loss stronger dependency within TP53-intact, research_signature_a nonnegative, research_signature_c negative; +0.582 [0.408,0.757]), H287/R265 accepts (PTEN-by-research_signature_a interaction within TP53-intact, research_signature_c negative; +0.563 [0.334,0.792]), H288/R266 accepts (PTEN-loss stronger dependency within TP53-intact, research_signature_a nonnegative, MSI-nonhigh; +0.546 [0.410,0.681]), and H289/R267 accepts (PTEN-by-research_signature_a interaction within TP53-intact, MSI-nonhigh; +0.515 [0.348,0.682]). Together these results close the key active question about whether the PTEN research_signature_a context depends on research_signature_c positivity or MSI-high overlap: the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency contrast remains large and threshold-clearing when research_signature_c is below its median and when MSI-high lines are excluded. H292/R270 accepts (SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency within PTEN-loss lines; +0.738 [0.535,0.942]), H293/R271 accepts (KEAP1-loss weaker dependency within PTEN-loss lines; +0.717 [0.519,0.915]), and H294/R272 accepts (TP53-loss weaker dependency within PTEN-loss lines; +0.559 [0.274,0.844]), showing that the robust core associations remain meaningful even after conditioning on PTEN-loss status. H290/R268 remains unresolved because TP53 loss within MSI-high lines has a positive signed point estimate (+0.301) consistent with weaker dependency but an imprecise interval crossing zero and below threshold (-0.129 to 0.732) with a small exposed cell (n=24). H291/R269 also remains unresolved because the MSI-high-by-TP53-loss interaction has a signed point estimate below threshold (+0.123) and a broad interval (-0.316 to 0.563); the interval does not clearly rule out a meaningful effect in the claimed direction. These imprecise results do not overturn the robust global MSI-high stronger-dependency association, but they maintain that the MSI-high/TP53-loss stratum is not dataset-internal resolved. Audit considerations: these are dataset-internal measured associations among measured predictors and constructed signatures, not causal knockout or mechanistic claims; research signatures and cutoffs are analysis-defined and possibly correlated; many subgroup/interaction tests have been explored, so nominal 95 percent intervals are not fully family-wise corrected. The accepted effects are large enough that chance alone is less plausible, but generalizability and biological interpretation remain unresolved. No validation request is made because independent validation capacity is exhausted (remaining_new_requests=0).",
  "research_notes": "Updated dataset-internal synthesis after appraisal of H286-H294. Validation capacity is exhausted and no validation was requested. Direction convention remains as registered: direction -1 means the signed positive estimate corresponds to a more negative exposed-minus-comparator dependency score (stronger dependency); direction +1 means a positive signed estimate corresponds to a less negative/more positive score (weaker dependency). Returned estimates/intervals were interpreted as already signed and not re-multiplied. Acceptance rule: valid Welch cells with each cell n>=20 and signed lower bound clearly >+0.15 composite dependency-score units in the registered direction; borderline lower bounds treated as unresolved, especially when cell counts are small. \n\nNewly accepted PTEN refinement: H286 and H287 show that the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency context tied to TP53-intact and research_signature_a nonnegative status extends into research_signature_c-negative backgrounds (R264 +0.582 [0.408,0.757]; R265 +0.563 [0.334,0.792]). H288 and H289 show the same PTEN context remains threshold-clear when MSI-high lines are excluded (R266 +0.546 [0.410,0.681]; R267 +0.515 [0.348,0.682]). This strengthens the narrow PTEN conclusion: PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency mainly in TP53-intact, research_signature_a-positive analysis-defined backgrounds; the association does not appear to be driven solely by overlap with high research_signature_c or MSI-high status. Still, these are dataset-internal, possibly overfitted subgroup contexts, not generalizable biomarker claims. \n\nNewly accepted robustness checks in PTEN-loss lines: H292 accepts SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency within PTEN-loss lines (+0.738 [0.535,0.942]); H293 accepts KEAP1-loss weaker dependency within PTEN-loss lines (+0.717 [0.519,0.915]); H294 accepts TP53-loss weaker dependency within PTEN-loss lines (+0.559 [0.274,0.844]). Thus conditioning on PTEN-loss status does not eliminate the strongest core dataset associations. \n\nNewly unresolved imprecise MSI-high/TP53-loss stratum: H290 TP53 loss within MSI-high lines is positive for the registered weaker-dependency direction but imprecise and includes negative values (+0.301 [-0.129,0.732], exposed n=24), so closed unresolved. H291 MSI-high-by-TP53-loss interaction has signed point below threshold and broad interval (+0.123 [-0.316,0.563]), also closed unresolved. These do not overturn global MSI-high stronger-dependency evidence but keep the TP53-loss MSI stratum ambiguous. \n\nOverall accepted core associations remain: SMARCA4 loss = stronger dependency; KEAP1 loss = weaker dependency; MSI-high = stronger dependency overall; TP53 loss = weaker dependency overall but attenuated/reversed in elevated research_signature_a and research_signature_b contexts; PTEN loss = context-specific stronger dependency concentrated in TP53-intact, research_signature_a-positive backgrounds, now shown robust to research_signature_c-negative and MSI-nonhigh exclusions. Negative/unsupported findings include lineage subtype, KRAS, MTAP, PIK3CA, MYC amplification, constructed markers D/E/F, STK11, ARID1A, RB1, BRCA2, NF1, sex, media serum, BRAF, EGFR/ERBB2 amplification, SMAD4, APC, screen batch, library/omics coverage contrasts, and many interactions that did not clear the threshold. All claims are dataset-internal measured associations only; causality, mechanism, single-gene interpretation, pathway interpretation, and external generalizability remain unsupported. The most useful remaining analysis would be pre-specified multivariable modeling or external validation if capacity existed; since validation is exhausted, accepted conclusions should remain context-conditional and not be presented as generalizable biomarker statements."
